CORE broken-cprover-smt-backend no-new-smt
main.i
--arch x86_64 --os linux --floatbv
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
Verifies that casting a finite `double` to `long double` and back yields
the original `double` bit-for-bit, and that sign is preserved across
the promotion.  Exercises both the constant-folding path (compile-time)
and the symbolic path (nondet input).

This test reads the bit pattern of a `double` via union aliasing
(`union { double d; uint64_t i; }`) AND casts that `double` to a
`long double` and back.  Two pre-existing limitations of the legacy
cprover-smt2 backend in FPA mode (`use_FPA_theory == true`) currently
block this test:

  1. Binary64 union read: `convert_expr(... typecast double -> bv64 ...)`
     reaches `flatten2bv`, which holds an invariant that floatbv
     expressions are already flattened by an earlier pass when FPA is
     in use.  No such pass exists for binary64 today.
  2. Mixed FPA/BV typecast: casting an FPA-encoded `double` to a
     BV-encoded `long double` (or back) routes through
     `convert_floatbv`, whose `float_bv.*` helper expects bit-vector
     operands -- the SMT-LIB emitted for the FPA-encoded source is
     mistyped.  Also affects programs that mix `double` and `long
     double` without the union read.  See the comment at the
     `floatbv_typecast`/`typecast` dispatch sites in
     `src/solvers/smt2/smt2_conv.cpp` for the implementation TODO.

Once both limitations are lifted, the broken-cprover-smt-backend tag
can be removed.  The new SMT2 backend has its own pre-existing
limitations and is excluded via no-new-smt.

The source is provided as a pre-processed `main.i` so CBMC parses it
without invoking the host preprocessor; combined with `--arch x86_64
--os linux`, this lets the test run on any host.
